# .public-surface-allow - justified exceptions to scripts/public-surface-check.
#
# One rule per line, four fields:
#
#     category | path-glob | reason | pattern
#
#   category    a deny category (private-repo, internal-infra, codename,
#               cluster-shape, credential-shape, secret-store, private-remote,
#               or a category this repository adds in .public-surface-deny)
#               or `*` for every category
#   path-glob   `*` for the whole repository, or a glob over the
#               repository-relative path. Use `COMMIT` for commit messages.
#   reason      required, at least 8 characters: why the text is legitimately
#               public, not what the pattern does.
#   pattern     POSIX extended regular expression. `%HH` is a hex escape.
#
# A rule masks exactly the text its pattern matches, only under the paths its
# glob matches, only for its category. Everything else on the same line is
# still checked. A rule broad enough to disable a category is rejected.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendored upstream sources, kept byte for byte from protovalidate, whose own
# doc comments carry RFC 1918 example addresses. Scoped to the vendored tree.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cluster-shape | */gen/buf/validate/* | vendored protovalidate sources ship these upstream example addresses verbatim and are regenerated, not edited | 192\.168\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tooling this repository legitimately documents.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cluster-shape | docs/* | the operator CLI workflow namespace flag, not a cluster namespace | %2d-namespace default

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# npm's lockfile identity for the pinned GitHub-hosted Git dependencies (the
# TypeScript projection's Invariant Protocol and the console UI's dashboard
# framework). npm records every GitHub-hosted Git dependency under this
# canonical URL whatever form package.json declares, and still installs a
# public host over HTTPS, so each lock is kept byte for byte as `npm install`
# writes it. The version check names the same form and pins it to the one SHA
# package.json declares. The guard matches lower-cased text after its fleet
# baseline masks public repository coordinates, so the patterns name only the
# scheme prefix.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
private-remote | package-lock.json | npm writes this canonical source for the pinned public dependency and clones it over HTTPS; hand-editing it would make the lock irreproducible | "resolved": "git\+%73sh://git@
private-remote | cmd/temporaless-console/ui/package-lock.json | npm writes this canonical source for the pinned public dashboard framework and clones it over HTTPS; hand-editing it would make the lock irreproducible | "resolved": "git\+%73sh://git@
private-remote | scripts/check_versions.py | the version check spells the exact lockfile source npm writes for the pinned public dependencies | git\+%73sh://git@[{]repository[}]#

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The optional console adapter serves the public terminal-core TerminalService
# contract (Apache-2.0), vendored byte for byte at its v0.6.0 tag. Its protobuf
# package and file paths are wire identity, so they cannot be renamed. These
# rules cover only the vendored tree, the Go bindings generated from it, and
# the template that maps its files; hand-written code never names the brand.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* | third_party/medallion-terminal-core/* | vendored upstream public contract kept byte for byte; its package name is wire identity | %6dedallion
* | adapters/go/console/internal/terminalv1/* | Go bindings generated from the vendored public contract repeat its package name and comments | %6dedallion
* | adapters/go/console/buf.gen.yaml | the generation template maps the vendored files by their upstream paths | %6dedallion/terminal/v1/
codename | third_party/medallion-terminal-core/* | upstream comments use the common noun for semantic object types, not a codename | %4fntology (object detail|type name|type of the target)
codename | adapters/go/console/internal/terminalv1/* | generated comments repeat the upstream common-noun wording | %4fntology (object detail|type name|type of the target)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shared guard's own text. Its fleet baseline has to name the public brand
# hostnames it excuses, and it writes them as a regular expression, so the
# escaped form is not covered by the baseline rule that masks the plain form.
# Scoped to that one file and that one span.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
brand-isolation | scripts/public-surface-check | the shared guard's baseline must name the public brand hostnames it allows fleet-wide; this is guard configuration, not repository prose | %6aimtech\\\.xyz\|%6dedallion\\\.%6aimtech\\\.io

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Considered and deliberately NOT given a rule. The guard already distinguishes
# these from the things it denies, and every one of them is in the known-good
# corpus the guard re-validates on every run, so masking them here would only
# create a hole:
#
#   codes.Internal / StatusCode.INTERNAL   the non-public-hostname pattern is
#       lower-case only, so a capitalised gRPC status code is not a hostname.
#   storage-key                            the key-file pattern is anchored to
#       a word boundary, so the tail of a longer identifier does not match.
#   logisticsOps                           likewise for the secret-store term.
#   run ids such as r2                     no category matches a bare run id.
#   example.com / example.org / .test /    RFC 2606 and RFC 6761 reserved names
#       .invalid / localhost               are allowed fleet-wide already.
#   127.0.0.1 / 0.0.0.0                    loopback and unspecified addresses
#       are allowed fleet-wide already.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
